## Problem

The MiniMax quota providers (`minimax-coding-plan` and `minimax-cn-coding-plan`, sharing `packages/web/server/lib/quota/providers/minimax-shared.js`) report **time-until-reset as `windowSeconds`** whenever the API payload lacks start/end timestamps. `windowSeconds` is the window *duration* everywhere else in the quota system; a remaining-time value shrinks as the window ages and misclassifies the window for every consumer that keys off the duration.

This is the same defect class as #495 (Cursor reporting time-until-reset as `windowSeconds`), in a different provider.

## Evidence (verified on main 029f69675)

`calculateWindowSeconds` in `minimax-shared.js:82-89`:

```js
const calculateWindowSeconds = (startAt, resetAt, remainsTimeMs) => {
  if (startAt && resetAt && resetAt > startAt) {
    return Math.floor((resetAt - startAt) / 1000);
  }
  if (remainsTimeMs && remainsTimeMs > 0) {
    return Math.floor(remainsTimeMs / 1000);
  }
  return null;
};
```

- The function's own header comment (`minimax-shared.js:78-80`) states `remains_time` is the time *remaining*: "MiniMax API returns remains_time in milliseconds (confirmed via live API testing: 9664502 ms = 2.68h in a 5h window, consistent with remaining_percent)". 2.68h is what is left of the 5h window, not its duration (18000s).
- The fallback feeds both windows: `intervalWindowSeconds` (:127) and `weeklyWindowSeconds` (:128), so a weekly window mid-period can report e.g. 3.5 days as its "duration".
- The existing test enshrines the wrong value — `minimax-shared.test.js:209-216` ("falls back to remains_time in milliseconds for window seconds") expects `windowSeconds` to be `9664` for `remains_time: 9_664_502` with 50% remaining in a 5h window. The correct duration is 18000; the correct answer when it cannot be derived is `null`.
- Correct precedents in the same codebase:
  - The VS Code port of this very provider (`packages/vscode/src/quotaProviders.ts`, `fetchMiniMaxQuota`) derives windowSeconds only from start/end timestamps and returns `null` otherwise — no remains_time fallback.
  - `neuralwatt.js:86` passes `null` "rather than fabricating a guess" when no window start is exposed.
  - #495 fixed the identical mislabel in the Cursor provider.

Consumers that trust the duration: `usageHeadline.ts` picks the shortest `windowSeconds` as the headline window, and `UsageGiftResetButton.tsx:22-25` classifies the reset type by exact duration match (18000 → FIVE_HOUR, 604800 → WEEK) — a remains-derived 9664 matches neither and the window silently loses its classification.

Dedup: searches for "minimax windowSeconds" and "remains_time" across open and closed issues return only #484 (minimax test-coverage, since resolved) and unrelated providers. No issue tracks this.

## Why it matters

The MiniMax 5h/weekly windows drive the quota headline and reset classification. Reporting a shrinking remainder as the duration makes the same window look shorter every poll, can promote/demote it in the headline ordering against other providers' true durations, and drops the gift-reset classification. It is silent: no error, just a wrong number that drifts.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `calculateWindowSeconds` no longer uses `remains_time` / `weekly_remains_time` as a duration: with no usable start/end pair, `windowSeconds` is `null` for both the interval and weekly windows
- [ ] The start/end derivation is unchanged (start/end present → `floor((resetAt - startAt) / 1000)`)
- [ ] `minimax-shared.test.js` updated: the remains_time fallback test (:209) now expects `null`, and the model-pick tests that assert `windowSeconds` 7200/3600 from remains-only payloads (:84-102) are updated to match; a remains-only weekly payload also reports `null`
- [ ] Do NOT substitute a hardcoded 18000/604800: the plan's real durations are not stated by the payload on this path (the VS Code port returns `null` here too)
- [ ] Existing minimax-shared tests pass otherwise (token-plan usage inversion, model-pick precedence, weekly suppression unchanged)

## Bug

`applyForwardProxyResponseHeaders` in `packages/web/server/proxy-headers.js` forwards every upstream response header with repeated `response.setHeader(key, value)` calls. Node's `setHeader` **replaces** an existing value, so when the upstream sends the same header more than once — above all `Set-Cookie`, the one header that is legitimately repeated — every value except the last is silently lost.

The helper is on the OpenCode proxy path (`packages/web/server/lib/opencode/proxy.js:664, 950, 1136, 1176`), so any upstream response that sets more than one cookie reaches the browser with only the final cookie.

## Evidence (verified on main `501f17168`, real Node `ServerResponse`)

```js
import http from 'node:http';
import { applyForwardProxyResponseHeaders } from './packages/web/server/proxy-headers.js';

const res = new http.ServerResponse(new http.IncomingMessage(null));
const h = new Headers();
h.append('set-cookie', 'a=1; Path=/');
h.append('set-cookie', 'b=2; Path=/');
h.set('content-type', 'text/plain');
applyForwardProxyResponseHeaders(h, res);

res.getHeader('set-cookie'); // => "b=2; Path=/"  — the a=1 cookie is gone
```

Note the existing test in `proxy-headers.test.js` uses a fake `response` whose `setHeader` pushes into an array, which hides the overwrite semantics of a real response object.

## Suggested fix

Accumulate repeated header values and set them as an array (Node/Express send arrays as separate header lines), at minimum for `set-cookie` — e.g. group values by lowercased key while iterating, use `responseHeaders.getSetCookie()` for the cookie header when available, then call `setHeader` once per key with the array when there is more than one value.

## Acceptance criteria

- A response proxied through `applyForwardProxyResponseHeaders` with two upstream `Set-Cookie` headers carries both cookies (`res.getHeader('set-cookie')` returns both values).
- Single-value headers and the existing filtered-header behavior (content-encoding, transfer-encoding, www-authenticate, etc. dropped) are unchanged.
- New regression test uses a real `http.ServerResponse` (or a fake whose setHeader replaces, matching Node semantics), not a push-array fake.
- Existing `packages/web/server/proxy-headers.test.js` tests pass.

# fix(web): worktree start-command timeout env reader has no upper bound — a value over 2^31-1 ms collapses the #861 timeout to ~1 ms and kills every start command instantly

## Evidence

`packages/web/server/lib/git/service.js:2154-2161` at main `389e42c52`:

```js
const worktreeStartCommandTimeoutMs = () => {
  const raw = String(process.env.OPENCHAMBER_WORKTREE_START_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS || '').trim();
  if (!/^\d+$/.test(raw)) {
    return WORKTREE_START_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS;
  }
  const parsed = Number(raw);
  return parsed > 0 ? parsed : WORKTREE_START_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS;
};
```

The spelling is strict (plain decimal only — good), but there is no upper bound and no safe-integer check. The value feeds the `timeout` option of both `execFileAsync` calls in `runWorktreeStartCommand` (`service.js:2181` Windows, `:2196` POSIX). Node's timer cannot represent a delay over 2^31-1 ms: it emits `TimeoutOverflowWarning` and sets the duration to 1 ms.

This reader was added by the #861 fix (PR #864, merged today) — it reintroduces the exact overflow class that #832 fixed in the older strict readers and that #873 tracks for the quota reader. The sibling readers all bound this: `opencode/self-handoff.js:36-41` caps at `2_147_483_647` with a comment explaining why, and `relay/host-client.js` `resolveBatchWindowMs` does the same.

### Verified this run (laptop probe vs the real module)

Probe: `~/git/fleet/probe-oc-worktree-timeout-20261029/probe.mjs` on the laptop, importing the real `createWorktree` / `getWorktreeBootstrapStatus` from `packages/web/server/lib/git/service.js` at `389e42c52`. Start command: a Node script that sleeps 2 s and then writes `start-marker.txt` in the worktree.

- Control (env unset, default 600_000 ms): bootstrap `setup-ready` in 2572 ms, marker written, no warnings.
- `OPENCHAMBER_WORKTREE_START_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS=3000000000`: Node printed `TimeoutOverflowWarning: 3000000000 does not fit into a 32-bit signed integer. Timeout duration was set to 1.` The start command was killed after ~1 ms, the marker was never written, and bootstrap finished in 616 ms with the failure logged as a bare `Worktree start command failed: Command failed: bash -lc ...`.

The masking is part of the bug: `failureMessage` (`service.js:2168-2173`) only reports a timeout when `Date.now() - startedAt >= timeoutMs`, which can never hold for the overflowed value, so the operator sees an unexplained "Command failed" for a command that would have succeeded.

## Why it matters

The env override exists so installs that legitimately run for minutes can be given room (and so tests can shrink it). An operator who sets a generous value above ~24.8 days in ms — or fat-fingers extra digits, e.g. `6000000000` — silently gets the opposite of what they asked for: every worktree start command is killed instantly, project setup (dependency installs, codegen) never runs, and the log message actively hides the cause. The failure looks like the user's own start command is broken.

## Acceptance criteria

- `worktreeStartCommandTimeoutMs()` rejects values above `2_147_483_647` (and non-safe-integers) by falling back to the 600_000 ms default, matching the `self-handoff.js` / `resolveBatchWindowMs` precedent — or clamps, as long as the effective timeout can never overflow; the chosen behaviour is named in a comment.
- Regression test in `packages/web/server/lib/git/service.test.js` next to the existing #861 hang test: an oversized env value (e.g. `3000000000`) no longer kills a short (~100 ms) start command — it completes (marker/output observed) under the default bound.
- Existing worktree bootstrap tests still pass, including the #861 hang test with env `1000`.
- No change to the default (600_000 ms), the strict plain-decimal spelling rule, or the kill semantics tracked separately in #868 (process-tree kill).

## Dedup

Checked open + closed issues 2026-10-30: #861 is the original no-timeout bug (closed by PR #864, which introduced this reader); #868 covers surviving descendant processes after the timeout kill — a different defect. #832 fixed this overflow class in the #698/#706/#714 readers; #873 tracks it for the quota CLI reader from PR #740. None covers the worktree start-command reader from PR #864.

