# fix(vscode): worktree start-command timeout env reader has no upper bound — a value over 2^31-1 ms collapses the timeout to ~1 ms and kills every VS Code worktree start command instantly

## Finding

PR #884 (commit `4309ecd0fa`, closing #881) bounded the **web** worktree start-command timeout reader at 2^31-1 ms, but the VS Code twin reader — which parses the same env var, `OPENCHAMBER_WORKTREE_START_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS` — was not touched and still has no upper bound and no safe-integer check.

## Evidence

Source PR reviewed: [PR #884](https://github.com/developing-today/openchamber/pull/884) — its diff changes only `packages/web/server/lib/git/service.js` and `packages/web/server/lib/git/service.test.js`.

Verified on current main (`163f002d3627a39c47b694ad36ed13e47159b430`):

`packages/vscode/src/gitService.ts:1553-1561`:

```ts
const WORKTREE_START_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS = 600_000;
const worktreeStartCommandTimeoutMs = (): number => {
  const raw = String(process.env.OPENCHAMBER_WORKTREE_START_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS || '').trim();
  if (!/^\d+$/.test(raw)) {
    return WORKTREE_START_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS;
  }
  const parsed = Number(raw);
  return parsed > 0 ? parsed : WORKTREE_START_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS;
};
```

The parsed value feeds a raw timer in the same file (`runWorktreeStartCommand`, ~line 1606):

```ts
const timeoutTimer = setTimeout(() => {
  timedOut = true;
  termination = terminate().catch(() => {});
  ...
}, timeoutMs);
```

A delay above 2,147,483,647 ms overflows Node/Bun timers to ~1 ms (`TimeoutOverflowWarning`) — exactly the failure #881 documented for the web copy. So an operator (or a config generator) who raises the VS Code timeout for a long install instead gets every worktree start command terminated after ~1 ms and reported as timed out, when no real timeout elapsed.

The web copy after #884 (`packages/web/server/lib/git/service.js:2185-2199`) instead requires `Number.isSafeInteger(parsed) && parsed > 0 && parsed <= 2_147_483_647`, falling back to the 600,000 ms default otherwise, and its test pins `'3000000000'` falling back (`service.test.js:2133`). The VS Code test file (`packages/vscode/src/gitService.worktree-bootstrap.test.js`) only ever sets the env var to `'1000'`; there is no oversize pin for this copy.

Dedupe: #881 was web-scoped in title and body and is closed by #884; no open or closed issue covers the VS Code reader (searched `worktreeStartCommandTimeoutMs` and worktree-timeout terms, 2026-10-31).

## Why it matters

This is the same operator-facing trap #881/#884 removed on the web side, left live on the VS Code side of the same feature: the two copies share the env var name, so a single (mis)configuration that is now safe for web worktrees silently breaks VS Code worktree creation instead. Cross-copy drift of a bound that was explicitly fixed once is likely to be rediscovered the hard way.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `worktreeStartCommandTimeoutMs` in `packages/vscode/src/gitService.ts` falls back to the 600,000 ms default for values above 2,147,483,647 and for non-safe-integer spellings, matching the web reader in `packages/web/server/lib/git/service.js`.
- [ ] A regression test in `packages/vscode/src/gitService.worktree-bootstrap.test.js` (or the module's test seam) pins: `2147483647` accepted, `3000000000` and `9007199254740993` falling back to 600,000.
- [ ] Existing worktree-bootstrap tests still pass in CI; the 600,000 ms default, plain-decimal spelling rule, and process-tree kill semantics (#868) are unchanged.

## Bug

The abandoned-lock reclaim in `packages/web/server/lib/source-control/file-lock.js` opens the existing lock file with a plain `'r'` open — no `O_NONBLOCK` — and only afterwards judges whether it is a regular file:

```js
// file-lock.js:136-142 (main 3d7ef01f)
async function reclaimAbandoned(fsImpl, lockPath) {
  try { handle = await fsImpl.open(lockPath, 'r'); } catch { return false; }
  try {
    const stats = await handle.stat({ bigint: true });
    if (!stats.isFile()) return false;              // unreachable for a FIFO
    const content = await handle.readFile('utf8');  // whole, no size check
```

```js
// file-lock.js:156-162 — the sync twin
  try { handle = fsImpl.openSync(lockPath, 'r'); } catch { return false; }
    const stats = fsImpl.fstatSync(handle, { bigint: true });
    if (!stats.isFile()) return false;
    const content = fsImpl.readFileSync(handle, 'utf8');
```

On POSIX, opening a FIFO with `O_RDONLY` blocks until a writer appears. The `isFile()` refusal is therefore unreachable for exactly the file type it exists to refuse:

- **Async** (`withSourceControlFileLock`, :185-189): the reclaim is awaited inside the acquire loop, so the caller's `waitMs` deadline (default 2 s) never gets a chance to fire — the lock call never settles at all, instead of settling with the coded `SOURCE_CONTROL_LOCK_BUSY` error. Every store built on this lock inherits the wedge: source-control mutation / binding / audit storage, GitHub auth, GitLab auth (including the `.renew.lock`), and the git contributor-provenance, network-operation, worktree-bootstrap and SSH-credential stores.
- **Sync** (`withSourceControlFileLockSync`, :236-240, used by `git/identity-storage.js` :244/:251): `openSync` on a FIFO blocks the whole server event loop — not one store, the entire process freezes with no error and no recovery.

The lock record itself is a single small JSON line (`ownerRecord()`), yet after the handle stat the content is also read whole with no size check (`:142` / `:162`) — `stats` is already in hand, so an oversized regular file at the lock path is buffered entire before `isAbandoned` ever parses it.

This is the same open-before-guard shape as #1701 (git hook inspection) and #1711 (SSH key snapshot): the shared bounded reader opens `O_RDONLY|O_NONBLOCK` and judges regular-file from the handle for exactly this reason (`shared/bounded-file-read.js:36`). Here it was missed because the readers under audit were the state files; the lock files that guard them were never dispositioned (#1663 covered mutation/audit/binding `readState`, not `file-lock.js`).

## Evidence

- `packages/web/server/lib/source-control/file-lock.js:138` — async reclaim open, plain `'r'`, no `O_NONBLOCK`; `:141` isFile judged after the open; `:142` `handle.readFile` whole with no cap.
- `packages/web/server/lib/source-control/file-lock.js:158` — sync reclaim `openSync(lockPath, 'r')`; `:161-162` same judge-after-open, `readFileSync` whole.
- Callers verified on main `3d7ef01f02587af6285fbb7ea8ab2b2129a67e8c`: `source-control/mutation-storage.js:180/:330`, `binding-storage.js:40`, `audit-storage.js:209`, `github/auth.js:246`, `gitlab/auth-storage.js:207/:253`, `git/contributor-provenance-storage.js:92`, `git/network-operation-storage.js:460`, `git/worktree-bootstrap-storage.js:180`, `git/ssh-credential-storage.js:51`, `git/identity-storage.js:244/:251` (sync).
- Laptop probe against the **real** module (laptop `~/git/fleet/probe-oc-file-lock-fifo-20261106/probe.mjs`), run twice with identical results:
  - async control (stale lock, dead pid): settled `ran` in 23 ms;
  - async FIFO at the lock path, `waitMs: 2000`: **NOT SETTLED after 4,503 ms** (both runs) — the 2 s deadline is defeated;
  - sync control: settled `ran` in 4 ms;
  - sync FIFO: the probe's 250 ms heartbeat stops the moment `openSync` is entered, no settled line, process killed by the external timeout (exit 124, both runs) — the event loop is frozen.
- Dedup: open + closed issue search for `file-lock`, `reclaimAbandoned`, "source-control lock" — no filing covers the lock file's own reclaim read. #1663/#1677 cover the state files these locks guard, a different read in different files.

## Why it matters

The reclaim path exists so a crashed owner's lock never wedges a store. As written, one non-regular file at any `.lock` path wedges that store's operations forever (async — silently, past the deadline that was supposed to bound the wait) and takes the whole server down on the sync identity-store path. The failure has no error surface: callers hang instead of receiving the coded busy/failed errors this module carefully defines.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Both reclaim opens cannot block on a non-regular file (`O_NONBLOCK` on POSIX, regular-file judged from the opened handle, as in `shared/bounded-file-read.js`); a FIFO at the lock path is refused promptly and treated as "not abandoned" / busy under the existing error contract — never a hang.
- [ ] The async acquire loop's `waitMs` deadline bounds the whole attempt again, including the reclaim: a FIFO lock settles with `SOURCE_CONTROL_LOCK_BUSY` within the deadline.
- [ ] The reclaim content read is judged against the already-statted handle size (or a named small cap fitting one owner record) before buffering; an oversized regular file at the lock path is not read whole.
- [ ] Normal behaviour unchanged: a stale lock from a dead local pid is still reclaimed and the operation runs; a live owner's lock still answers busy after `waitMs`; guard-file and release semantics untouched.
- [ ] Regression tests pin a FIFO at the lock path for **both** the async and sync entry points, settling promptly (both hang on pre-fix code); existing `file-lock` and dependent store suites pass.

## Summary

When a UI password is configured, `createUiAuth` loads the UI-auth JWT secret from `<OPENCHAMBER_DATA_DIR>/jwt-secret` with a bare synchronous whole-file read — no size cap, no regular-file check, and no deadline:

```js
// packages/web/server/lib/ui-auth/ui-auth.js:437-445 (main 4bffa902)
function getOrCreateJwtSecret() {
  const envSecret = process.env.OPENCODE_JWT_SECRET;
  if (envSecret) {
    return new TextEncoder().encode(envSecret);
  }

  try {
    if (fs.existsSync(JWT_SECRET_FILE)) {
      return new TextEncoder().encode(fs.readFileSync(JWT_SECRET_FILE, 'utf8').trim());
    }
  } catch (e) {
    console.warn('[JWT] Failed to read secret file:', e.message);
  }
  // …otherwise generate + persist a fresh 64-char hex secret
}
```

The `try/catch` only handles a *throwing* read. A read that never returns is not a throw. `JWT_SECRET_FILE` is `path.join(OPENCHAMBER_DATA_DIR, 'jwt-secret')` (:435), and the read runs synchronously inside `createUiAuth` (:695) on the password-configured path.

## Verified behaviour (laptop probe against the real module, run twice, identical)

Probe: `~/git/fleet/probe-oc-jwt-secret-20261107/` (`probe.mjs`) — sets `OPENCHAMBER_DATA_DIR` to a fresh temp dir (the same seam `ui-auth.test.js` uses), imports the real `ui-auth.js`, and calls the real `createUiAuth({ password: 'secret' })`, then issues a real session token via `handleSessionCreate` and verifies it externally with `jose`:

- **Control** — `jwt-secret` containing a normal 64-char secret: `createUiAuth` settled in ~31–37 ms; the issued session token verified against the file's exact contents.
- **Oversize** — a 33 MiB file (33,554,432 `A`s + a tail marker): settled in ~231–246 ms, and the issued token **verified against the file's full 33 MiB contents** as the HS256 key and did **not** verify against its first 64 chars — the whole file is buffered and accepted as the signing key. A real secret this server writes is 64 hex chars.
- **FIFO** — a FIFO at the `jwt-secret` path: `createUiAuth` **never settled**; both runs had to be killed by the outer 8 s `timeout` (exit 124). `existsSync` answers true for a FIFO, so execution reaches the sync read and the whole server process freezes inside it.

## Why it matters

- This is a **synchronous** read on the server startup / auth-setup path: one FIFO (or any never-ending file) at `jwt-secret` freezes the entire server before it can serve anything, with no warning — the existing `console.warn` only fires on a thrown error, and a blocked `readFileSync` never throws.
- The oversize half is the standard unbounded-read exposure: an arbitrary-size file at the secret path is buffered whole into memory and used as the HMAC key.
- On the inventory exception: `lib/shared/DOCUMENTATION.md:379` excepts this reader with the reason "a raw secret file, not a JSON store; it is read as bytes for a key, and no disposition question arises". That reason answers the *disposition* half only — it never addresses the *bound* half. A FIFO does not degrade to "absent" here (the degrade-to-absent outcome the foreign-file exceptions at :378 rely on); it hangs, which is exactly the ground on which the sibling exceptions for the mcpx daemon discovery (#1773) and the Muse login store (#1774) were overridden. The APNs `.p8` key file (#1777) is the same shape — a raw key file read as bytes for a key — and is being bounded for the same reason.

Not covered by existing issues: #1656's inventory audit recorded the exception row above and filed nothing for this reader; #1613/#1609 bound other secret-bearing stores (quota credentials, environment). No open issue or PR touches `ui-auth.js`'s secret read.

## Acceptance criteria

- The `jwt-secret` read goes through a bounded, regular-file-checked read (e.g. the shared `lib/shared/bounded-file-read.js` sync reader, `O_NONBLOCK` on POSIX) with a small explicit cap appropriate for a 64-char hex secret (anything in the 1–16 KiB range is generous), enforced during the read, not only on a pre-read stat.
- A FIFO / non-regular file at the `jwt-secret` path makes `getOrCreateJwtSecret()` settle promptly, treated exactly like today's unreadable file: warn and fall through to the generate-and-persist path — server startup must never hang on the secret file.
- An over-cap file is refused the same way (not buffered whole, not accepted as the key).
- Existing behaviour is preserved for the normal cases: a valid secret file is honoured exactly as today (including the `.trim()`), a missing file still generates and persists a fresh secret, `OPENCODE_JWT_SECRET` (env) is untouched, and `rotateJwtSecret` / `persistJwtSecret` write behaviour is unchanged.
- Regression tests in `ui-auth.test.js` pin: valid file honoured, oversize file refused without buffering whole, and non-regular/FIFO-shaped read refused — mirroring the grows-after-stat / FIFO coverage the sibling bounded readers carry.

## Summary

In direct mode, `resolveApnsConfig` reads the APNs signing key from the file named by `OPENCHAMBER_APNS_P8_PATH` with a bare whole-file read — no size cap, no regular-file check, and no deadline:

```js
