# fix(web): gatekeeper control body reader decodes per chunk and judges its 1 MiB byte cap in chars — a split multi-byte character is corrupted in control values, and a 1,200,037-byte body was accepted past CONTROL_BODY_BYTES

## Evidence (verified this run, on main 8f61a0fd3f9849606a6d976b824431f2bd82ce36)

`packages/web/server/lib/spaces/gatekeeper-program.cjs` — the program of the gatekeeper container, a space's only way out, which holds every grant secret in memory — reads control request bodies in `readBody` (:771-785):

```js
const readBody = (request) => new Promise((resolve) => {
  let text = '';
  let tooLarge = false;
  request.on('data', (chunk) => {
    if (tooLarge) return;
    text += chunk;                                        // :777 per-chunk Buffer->string decode
    if (text.length > CONTROL_BODY_BYTES) {               //      judged in UTF-16 chars
      tooLarge = true;
      text = '';
    }
  });
  ...
});
```

against the byte-named cap at :96:

```js
const CONTROL_BODY_BYTES = 1024 * 1024;
```

Two defects in one reader:

1. `text += chunk` coerces each `Buffer` chunk to a string independently. Node gives no chunk-boundary guarantee, so a multi-byte UTF-8 character split across two chunks decodes to replacement characters — the same defect class as #1690/#1691/#1697, in the one reader those fixes did not cover. There is no `StringDecoder` anywhere in this file.
2. The cap named `CONTROL_BODY_BYTES` is compared against `text.length` (UTF-16 code units), so a body of multi-byte characters passes up to ~3x the stated byte cap before `tooLarge` fires.

### Probe (laptop, against the real program spawned exactly as `gatekeeper-program.test.js` spawns it; two runs, identical results both times)

Probe dir (laptop): `~/git/fleet/probe-oc-gatekeeper-readbody-20261106/` (`probe.js`, raw-socket POSTs to the control listener).

- Control, single write — `POST /network` with body `{"mode":"allowlist","domains":["€xample.com"]}`:
  `400 {"error":"'€xample.com' is not a domain name. ..."}` — the name echoed intact.
- Same body, written in two parts split inside the € sign's 3 UTF-8 bytes (250 ms apart):
  `400 {"error":"'���xample.com' is not a domain name. ..."}` — the value the program parsed was corrupted before any validation saw it.
- Byte cap — `POST /network` body `{"mode":"open","domains":[],"pad":"<400,000 × €>"}`: 1,200,037 bytes on the wire, 400,037 chars:
  `200 {"ok":true}` — accepted, ~152 KB past the 1 MiB byte cap.
- ASCII control — same field with 1,100,000 × `a` (over the cap in both measures):
  `400 {"error":"the body is not JSON"}` — the too-large path fired as designed.

`gatekeeper-program.test.js` has no coverage of `readBody`'s cap or decoding (no test references `CONTROL_BODY_BYTES`, the too-large path, or a split multi-byte control body).

## Why it matters

The control listener is the host's channel for setting the network allowlist and for grants — including grant secrets, which `addGrant` (:841-860) validates by length only, with no charset pattern. A chunk boundary inside a multi-byte character of a secret or header value silently stores a corrupted credential: the window then injects the wrong secret upstream and the failure presents as a bad credential at the provider, not as a gatekeeper defect. Separately, the stated 1 MiB bound on this channel under-enforces by up to ~3x for non-ASCII bodies, in the program whose own header treats everything it receives as hostile input and sizes its caps deliberately.

## Acceptance criteria

- `readBody` decodes the body as one UTF-8 stream (accumulate `Buffer`s and decode once, or use `StringDecoder`), so a multi-byte character split across any chunk boundary survives intact.
- The cap is judged in bytes (accumulated chunk lengths / `Buffer.byteLength`), so a body over 1,048,576 bytes is refused via the existing too-large path regardless of its character count, and accumulation stops growing once the cap is crossed.
- Regression tests in `gatekeeper-program.test.js`: (a) a control body with a multi-byte character split across writes is parsed with the value intact (e.g. the `/network` refusal echoes the name uncorrupted, or a grant field round-trips); (b) a body over the byte cap but under it in chars is refused; (c) the existing ASCII over-cap and valid-body behaviors are unchanged.
- No change to the control API, the cap value, or any other listener's behavior.

